---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Operating Model"
  description="How SCSDA is organised: research groups join with their own teams under a shared framework, projects sign through the academy, and a small in-house team runs delivery, finance and commercialisation."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Operating Model</p>
        <h2>A framework research groups choose to join.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        SCSDA was not built by hiring a large staff. It was built as an
        arrangement that research groups had a reason to join, and the
        arrangement did the work. This page sets out how the academy is
        organised, because the model itself is part of what the academy
        offers to prospective partners.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section surface">
    <div class="shell section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">How It Works</p>
        <h2>Five elements of the model.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Each element is designed so that participation is worthwhile for the
        joining group on its own terms, rather than depending on reporting
        lines or institutional obligation.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="shell output-grid">
      <article class="output-card">
        <h3>1. Groups join with their own teams</h3>
        <ul class="clean-list">
          <li>Academics join together with the researchers they already lead.</li>
          <li>They plug into a shared framework: research directions, task
            objectives, funding and indicators, and access to equipment,
            instruments and space.</li>
          <li>No one transfers employment to the academy.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="output-card">
        <h3>2. Projects sign through the academy</h3>
        <ul class="clean-list">
          <li>External projects won by participating groups are contracted in
            the academy's name.</li>
          <li>This gives the platform a coherent delivery record rather than a
            scatter of individual engagements.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="output-card">
        <h3>3. A small in-house team runs operations</h3>
        <ul class="clean-list">
          <li>The academy directly employs a core team covering finance,
            technical delivery, management, market development and
            commercialisation.</li>
          <li>Research groups are freed from administration; the platform keeps
            delivery discipline in one place.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="output-card">
        <h3>4. Mature results can spin out</h3>
        <ul class="clean-list">
          <li>Where results reach commercial maturity, staff can establish a
            company around them.</li>
          <li>The academy takes an equity holding and continues to support the
            venture rather than stepping away from it.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="output-card">
        <h3>5. Centres with European partners run the same model</h3>
        <ul class="clean-list">
          <li>Joint centres established with European partner institutions follow
            the same framework.</li>
          <li>Each rests on its own dated, signed agreement rather than an
            informal contact &mdash; four European universities across three
            countries, with instruments signed between December 2019 and
            December 2021.</li>
          <li>Cooperation funding flows in both directions: the academy pays the
            partner side for its participation.</li>
          <li>The traffic is two-way in substance, not only in wording: joint
            publications, four signed tripartite agreements for joint master's
            and doctoral supervision, and a shared submission to a European
            national research council.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The Model in Physical Form</p>
        <h2>Centres formed under the framework.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        The clearest evidence that the framework works is physical: research
        centres with their own premises, equipment and direction, formed by
        groups that chose to join.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="shell highlight-grid">
      <article class="case-card">
        <img
          src="/images/centres-development-wall.jpg"
          alt="Development wall of the Academy's research centres showing the staged centre-building programme"
          width="1080"
          height="810"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <div class="case-body">
          <span class="category">Platform structure</span>
          <h3>A staged centre-building programme</h3>
          <p>The Academy's centre wall records the development path: which centres were formed, in which phase, and around which research questions. Structure, not slogans.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="case-card">
        <img
          src="/images/mems-sensing-centre.jpg"
          alt="Entrance of the Smart MEMS Sensing Research Centre and the sensor environmental reliability laboratory"
          width="1080"
          height="810"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <div class="case-body">
          <span class="category">Research centres</span>
          <h3>Smart MEMS sensing and reliability testing</h3>
          <p>The Smart MEMS Sensing Research Centre and the sensor environmental reliability laboratory are two of the twelve joint bilateral centres formed during the construction period.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section surface">
    <div class="shell section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Advisory Structure</p>
        <h2>An expert committee that reviews the research, not the brochure.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Alongside the delivery framework sits an expert advisory committee of
        more than thirty professors and senior practitioners, co-chaired by an
        international academic and a Chinese industry expert, with vice-chairs
        drawn from both sides.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="shell output-grid">
      <article class="output-card">
        <h3>Five expert groups</h3>
        <ul class="clean-list">
          <li>Digital transformation of the construction sector</li>
          <li>Applied artificial intelligence for urban engineering records and management</li>
          <li>Intelligent sensing: MEMS, optical and AIoT sensors</li>
          <li>Intelligent construction and operations for buildings and infrastructure</li>
          <li>Green and low-carbon development: materials, energy and environment</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="output-card">
        <h3>What the committee does</h3>
        <ul class="clean-list">
          <li>Reviews research directions and topic selection before resources are committed.</li>
          <li>Advises on resource allocation, research quality and industrial fit.</li>
          <li>Connects the platform to sector needs and external collaboration.</li>
          <li>Provides advisory and review services to external organisations.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell article narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why This Matters</p>
      <h2>The arrangement is the capability.</h2>
      <p>
        A research platform that depends on direct authority can only grow as
        fast as it can hire. A platform built as a framework grows whenever a
        capable group finds it worth joining. During its construction period
        the academy developed twelve joint bilateral research centres and
        laboratories, each formed under a dated signed agreement with its
        co-founding partner, and cumulatively recruited forty-five researchers
        at master's level and above, alongside forty-six postgraduate and
        doctoral participants and a visiting professorship network of seventeen
        senior experts engaged under institutional agreements.
      </p>
      <p>
        These figures come from the government-accepted construction-period
        report covering 2019 to 2023. They are presented here to show how the
        model performed, not as a claim about current staffing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section surface">
    <div class="shell article narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Working With Us</p>
      <h2>Who this model suits.</h2>
      <p>
        The framework works best for research groups that already have their
        own people and direction, and that want delivery infrastructure around
        their work: contracting, finance, industry access and a route to
        commercialisation. It also suits European partner institutions that want
        a working relationship in China with defined funding flows and a dated
        signed instrument, rather than a memorandum of understanding.
      </p>
      <p>
        Individual cooperation agreements are held with academics at a number
        of universities. The academy does not present these as
        institution-level partnerships, and does not list partner names
        publicly, since an agreement with a researcher is not an endorsement
        by their employer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
